# 0005. One config for macOS and Arch, guarded per OS and per tool

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-11

The same files run on the Mac and on Arch; differences are handled inline, and every helper
works when its tool is missing.

## Technical details
- **Problem**: the same shell runs on a Mac (BSD userland, Homebrew) and an Arch desktop (GNU,
  Wayland or X11), and not every tool is installed on both.
- **OS detection**: `conf.d/00-env.zsh` sets `IS_MAC` / `IS_LINUX` from `$OSTYPE` once. On
  macOS it adds `/opt/homebrew/{bin,sbin}`, keg-only `libpq` (with `LDFLAGS`/`CPPFLAGS` for
  psycopg builds) and Homebrew's `site-functions` to fpath.
- **Order of checks**: `(( IS_MAC ))` / `[[ $OSTYPE == darwin* ]]`, then `$WAYLAND_DISPLAY`,
  then X11. Example, the clipboard aliases: `cbc`/`cbp` are `pbcopy`/`pbpaste`, else
  `wl-copy`/`wl-paste`, else `xclip -selection clipboard`.
- **Tool guards**: every alias and helper is wrapped in `(( $+commands[x] ))`; the cache
  helpers return early when the tool is missing, so a fresh machine with few tools still
  starts cleanly and silently.
- **Portable flags**: BSD and GNU differ (`sed -i.bak` then `rm`, not `sed -i`; `script`
  syntax in `make check` is chosen by `uname`). Flags are checked on both before use.
- **Packages**: `scripts/install-deps.sh` installs the same tool set with brew or pacman;
  `--devops` adds kubectl, helm, k9s, terraform, aws, docker, yq.
- **Consequences**: one place to change; every helper needs a guard and BSD/GNU care.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: Homebrew (macOS), pacman (Arch), wl-clipboard / xclip.
- Alternatives: separate mac/linux files or branches: drift, every change made twice.
